[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Lambda Expressions and LINQ in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial provides a comprehensive guide to using Lambda Expressions and LINQ (Language Integrated Query) in C#. LINQ allows you to query data from various sources using a consistent syntax, while Lambda Expressions provide a concise way to represent anonymous functions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Lambda Expressions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Lambda Expression is an anonymous function that can be treated as a value. It's a shorthand way of writing a method without a declaration (i.e., no access modifier, return type, or name). The basic syntax is (input parameters) => expression or statement block."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Example: A lambda expression that squares a number
Func<int, int> square = x => x * x;
int result = square(5); // result will be 25"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'Func<int, int>' is a delegate type that represents a function taking an integer as input and returning an integer. 'x => x * x' is the lambda expression. 'x' is the input parameter, and 'x * x' is the expression that calculates the square of 'x'."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"LINQ (Language Integrated Query)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"LINQ provides a unified way to query data from different data sources, such as collections, databases, and XML. It uses a set of standard query operators that can be chained together to perform complex queries."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"LINQ with Lambda Expressions"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lambda expressions are commonly used with LINQ to define the conditions and transformations applied to the data. Here are some common LINQ methods used with lambda expressions:"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Where: Filters a sequence of values based on a predicate (a function that returns a boolean)."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"List<int> numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 };
IEnumerable<int> evenNumbers = numbers.Where(x => x % 2 == 0); // Filters for even numbers"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select: Projects each element of a sequence into a new form."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"List<string> names = new List<string> { "Alice", "Bob", "Charlie" };
IEnumerable<int> nameLengths = names.Select(name => name.Length); // Gets the length of each name"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OrderBy: Sorts the elements of a sequence in ascending order."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"List<int> numbers = new List<int> { 5, 2, 8, 1, 9 };
IEnumerable<int> sortedNumbers = numbers.OrderBy(x => x); // Sorts the numbers in ascending order"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"GroupBy: Groups the elements of a sequence according to a specified key selector function."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"List<string> words = new List<string> { "apple", "banana", "apricot", "blueberry" };
var groupedWords = words.GroupBy(word => word[0]); // Groups words by their first letter"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Query Syntax vs. Method Syntax"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"LINQ offers two syntaxes: Query Syntax (similar to SQL) and Method Syntax (using extension methods). Both are equivalent and can be used interchangeably. Method syntax often uses lambda expressions."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// Query Syntax
List<int> numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 };
var evenNumbersQuery = from num in numbers
                       where num % 2 == 0
                       select num;

// Method Syntax (using Lambda Expression)
var evenNumbersMethod = numbers.Where(num => num % 2 == 0);"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Combining LINQ and Lambda Expressions"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"List<string> names = new List<string> { "Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "David", "Eve" };

// Find names that start with 'A' and have a length greater than 3
IEnumerable<string> filteredNames = names
    .Where(name => name.StartsWith("A") && name.Length > 3)
    .OrderBy(name => name);

foreach (string name in filteredNames)
{
    Console.WriteLine(name);
} // Output: Alice"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lambda Expressions and LINQ are powerful features in C# that enable concise and expressive data manipulation. Mastering these concepts will significantly improve your ability to write efficient and readable code when working with collections and other data sources."}]
